  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 400 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nFor this afternoon, gusty winds and low relative humidity will\ncontinue in eastern ND, the Red River Valley, into west-central\nMN before sunset. While current observations of gusts in the\n25-35 mph range juxtaposed with RH values in the 20s would\nequate to critical fire weather conditions, current intel on\nfine fuel status reveals that grasses are not quite cured to a\nlevel that would promote rapid spread of fire. Thus, we continue\nto not host any dedicated messaging toward fire weather impacts.\n\nOtherwise this afternoon, increased chance for showers with \nembedded thunder near Lake of the Woods based on current trends \nin satellite and radar. Isolated showers/weak thunderstorms will\nremain possible until near sunset.\n\nThis is in response to strong Pacific Jet nosing out of the\nCanadian Rockies into the Northern Plains offering a belt of\nincreased winds amid dry air mass. Tomorrow into Friday, the jet\nmigrates east of our area with upper ridging building over the\nregion. This will continue to promote dry and above average\ntemperatures through at least Saturday. Saturday will be\nparticularly warm day with high temperatures well into the 80s\nin southeast ND and west-central MN. There is even some\nopportunity for even higher temperatures pushing into the\nupper 80s in the southern Red River Valley.\n\nAround Sunday, flow becomes more southwesterly aloft in response\nto upper troughing over the Pacific Coast, and eventually the\nIntermountain West next week. This will promote increasing \nmoisture into our region. Ensemble guidance generally agrees in\na stationary front lingering near our region late weekend into\nnext week. Exiting the upper trough will likely be at least a\ncouple of shortwave troughs that move through the Northern\nPlains offering forcing for ascent near the stationary front.\nThis will promote increased chances for rain starting around\nSunday, lasting through much of next week.\n\nConfidence is not high in details in precipitation evolution\nnext week however. Timing of more robust shortwave troughs\nresulting in more consolidated bouts of rain remain in question.\nThere is even a cluster of guidance that offers the season's\nfirst snow mid to late next week within the Northern Plains -\nbut again, confidence in this scenario is very low due to high\nspread in ensemble guidance. Ultimately, the upcoming pattern \nnext week will trend wetter with temperatures closer to seasonal\naverages.",
